  "answer_text": "\"...those owned by wealthy rulers.\"\n\nThis quote is attributed to the famous 14th-century North African scholar, historian, and philosopher **Ibn Khaldun**. It appears in his monumental 1377 work, ***The Muqaddimah*** (also known as the *Prolegomena* or *Introduction* to his universal history).\n\n### Context and Meaning\nIn *The Muqaddimah*, Ibn Khaldun laid down pioneering foundations for sociology, historiography, and economics. When discussing the relationship between the state and the economy, he argued strongly against the state or ruling elites engaging directly in commerce. \n\nThis quote highlights several of his key economic insights:\n\n1. **The Inefficiency of State Monopolies:** Ibn Khaldun observed that when rulers or wealthy elites engage in business, they inevitably use their political power, authority, and military might to manipulate the market. They might force citizens to buy from them, fix prices, or tax competitors out of business. Because they do not have to compete on a level playing field, they lack the incentive to remain efficient, innovative, or organized.\n2. **The Power of Private Enterprise:** In contrast, professional merchants must rely entirely on their own discipline, organization, planning, and reputation to survive. They must satisfy their customers, manage risk carefully, and adapt to supply and demand. \n3. **The Decline of Coercive Wealth:** Over time, the structural inefficiencies, corruption, and market distortions caused by state-run enterprises lead to economic stagnation and decline. Eventually, the sustainable, highly organized, and adaptive nature of private, professional commerce will outlast and surpass the artificial wealth generated by ruling elites.\n\nThis insight is widely considered an early precursor to modern free-market economic theory, predating Adam Smith\u2019s *The Wealth of Nations* by several centuries, by arguing that economic prosperity thrives when rulers focus on governance and leave commerce to the merchants.",
